import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Zero-Touch Network Device Provisioning Lab · San Lee"
  description="An end-to-end network automation lab that takes a factory-blank MikroTik device to a verified configuration after one power cycle."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/projects/netops-lab.html"
  ogType="article"
  ogImage="/assets/og-netops-lab.png"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* Wipe-to-provision topology (portfolio ADR-004). Page-scoped: the
       argument is this lab's closed loop, not a shared diagram type. Max-width
       260 keeps viewBox units 1:1 with CSS px on every gate width (same
       measured discipline as .backedge / .split-map). */
    .lab-topo {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 260px;
      height: auto;
      margin: 18px 0 6px;
    }
    .lab-topo .lt-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--rule-strong);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .lab-topo .lt-id {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    .lab-topo .lt-role {
      fill: var(--text-secondary);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
    }
    .lab-topo .lt-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    /* Sole human edge: solid accent, dual-encoded with a filled mark + BY HAND
       (matches .cycle [data-actor=hand] / .cycle-actor). Not oxide — oxide is
       reversal on this site. */
    .lab-topo .lt-hand {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .lab-topo .lt-hand-mark { fill: var(--accent); stroke: none; }
    .lab-topo .lt-hand-label {
      fill: var(--accent);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
    }
    .lab-topo .lt-hand-noun {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
    }
    /* Return edge: re-arm closes the loop. Accent + dasharray like L4's
       be-back so it reads as the load-bearing cycle, not another cable. */
    .lab-topo .lt-return {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      stroke-dasharray: 4 3;
    }
    .lab-topo .lt-note {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 12px;
    }
    .lab-topo .lt-link-label {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 10px;
    }
    .lab-topo marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    .lab-topo marker path.lt-head-hand { fill: var(--accent); }
    .lab-topo marker path.lt-head-return { fill: var(--accent); }
    .lab-topo-caption {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 0.92rem;
      margin: 0 0 6px;
    }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

  <SiteNav current="work" />


  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="../index.html">&larr; Back</a>
    <h1>Zero-Touch Provisioning</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A factory-blank router configures itself on one power cycle. One command, about ninety seconds.</p>

    <p class="lead">
      This is a physical lab: one MikroTik router, one Raspberry Pi, on a desk.
      The goal was a reliable wipe-to-provisioned cycle &mdash; power on a blank
      device, touch nothing, and have it come up fully configured. That cycle
      is the apparatus for a later experiment: an agent severing its own
      management access. Without a cheap way back, every failed run is a
      manual rebuild.
    </p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-labelledby="toc-heading">
      <p class="toc-heading" id="toc-heading">On this page</p>
      <ol>
      <li><a href="#the-result">The result</a></li>
      <li><a href="#the-finding-the-barriers-were-on-the-host-not">The finding: the barriers were on the host, not the device</a></li>
      <li><a href="#three-back-doors-one-shape">Three back doors, one shape</a></li>
      <li><a href="#a-deferred-item-that-turned-out-not-to-be">A deferred item that turned out not to be deferrable</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-this-does-not-establish">What this does not establish</a></li>
      <li><a href="#stack">Stack</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-it-demonstrates">What it demonstrates</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>
    <h2 id="the-result">The result</h2>
    <p>
      One command, one power cycle, about ninety seconds. No console cable, no
      serial adapter, no reset-button timing.
    </p>

    <!-- Topology of the wipe-to-provision lab. The ordered list below still
         carries the five steps; this figure's job is the closed loop a
         straight list cannot draw: re-arm returns into the next wipe, and
         power is the only hand in the apparatus. No 1–5 numbering here —
         sequence stays on ol.cycle (ADR-004 Decision 5 floor). -->
    <svg class="lab-topo" viewBox="0 0 260 150" role="img"
         aria-label="Wipe-to-provision lab: Raspberry Pi Netinstall host linked to the router over an isolated Ethernet path for BOOTP and TFTP; the only human step is power-cycling the router; the router’s final re-arm step closes the loop so the next wipe can start.">
      <title>Wipe-to-provision lab: Raspberry Pi Netinstall host linked to the router over an isolated Ethernet path for BOOTP and TFTP; the only human step is power-cycling the router; the router’s final re-arm step closes the loop so the next wipe can start.</title>
      <defs>
        <marker id="lt-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
        <marker id="lt-arrow-start" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="1" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
        <marker id="lt-arrow-hand" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
          <path class="lt-head-hand" d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
        <marker id="lt-arrow-return" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
          <path class="lt-head-return" d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <!-- Sole human edge: Power → Router. Filled mark + BY HAND dual-encode
           actor=hand the same way the cycle list does. Labels sit left of the
           mark so the tracked uppercase does not overflow the 260 viewBox. -->
      <text class="lt-hand-noun" x="196" y="12" text-anchor="end">Power</text>
      <circle class="lt-hand-mark" cx="204" cy="10" r="4" />
      <path class="lt-hand" d="M 204 15 L 204 36" marker-end="url(#lt-arrow-hand)" />
      <text class="lt-hand-label" x="196" y="30" text-anchor="end">By hand</text>

      <!-- Two boxes only — schematic hosts, not photoreal hardware. rx matches
           --radius-sm (2px); at max-width 260 viewBox units are CSS px 1:1. -->
      <rect class="lt-box" x="6" y="40" width="100" height="48" rx="2" />
      <text class="lt-id" x="56" y="60" text-anchor="middle">Pi</text>
      <text class="lt-role" x="56" y="76" text-anchor="middle">Netinstall</text>

      <rect class="lt-box" x="154" y="40" width="100" height="48" rx="2" />
      <text class="lt-id" x="204" y="60" text-anchor="middle">Router</text>
      <text class="lt-role" x="204" y="76" text-anchor="middle">wipe &#8594; cfg</text>

      <!-- Isolated Ethernet: Pi ↔ Router carries BOOTP/TFTP. Muted automated
           stroke — not the hand edge, not the return. -->
      <path class="lt-edge" d="M 108 64 L 150 64"
            marker-start="url(#lt-arrow-start)"
            marker-end="url(#lt-arrow)" />
      <text class="lt-link-label" x="129" y="56" text-anchor="middle">BOOTP / TFTP</text>

      <!-- Return edge under the pair: router re-arm → next wipe on the Pi
           side. Same closed-loop pattern as split-map A-return / L4 be-back. -->
      <path class="lt-return" d="M 204 90 C 204 128, 56 128, 56 92"
            marker-end="url(#lt-arrow-return)" />
      <text class="lt-note" x="130" y="144" text-anchor="middle">re-arm closes the next wipe</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="lab-topo-caption">
      Power is the only hand in the loop. Netinstall, the blank-router offer,
      OS and default config, and re-arm are automated; re-arm is the return
      edge that makes the next wipe start without rebuilding the host.
    </p>

    <ol class="cycle" aria-label="One wipe-to-provisioned cycle">
      <li>
        <p class="cycle-step"><span class="cycle-num">1</span>Start Netinstall on the Pi</p>
        <p class="cycle-note">It carries its own BOOTP and TFTP server, so nothing else has to be running.</p>
      </li>
      <li data-actor="hand">
        <p class="cycle-step"><span class="cycle-num">2</span>Pull the power, plug it back in<span class="cycle-actor">by hand</span></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="cycle-step"><span class="cycle-num">3</span>The router offers itself, Netinstall answers</p>
        <p class="cycle-note">It tries Ethernet once on boot, then falls through to its own flash if nothing answers.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="cycle-step"><span class="cycle-num">4</span>Fresh OS, then our default configuration</p>
        <p class="cycle-note">A management address, a firewall, and an SSH key it has never seen before.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="cycle-step"><span class="cycle-num">5</span>It re-arms itself for the next run</p>
        <p class="cycle-note">The last statement in the config script, deliberately: if it is refused, everything else has already applied.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="cycle-close">
      Exactly one of the five needs a person: pulling the power and plugging it
      back in.
    </p>
    <p>
      The provisioning host is the Pi rather than my desktop, so the whole cycle
      is scriptable from the box that is already always-on. MikroTik&rsquo;s
      Netinstall tool ships as a 32-bit x86 binary, and the Pi is ARM, so it
      runs under user-mode QEMU. The binary is statically linked, so there is
      no 32-bit sysroot to assemble.
    </p>

    <h2 id="the-finding-the-barriers-were-on-the-host-not">The finding: the barriers were on the host, not the device</h2>
    <p>
      After a fresh install, RouterOS leaves the admin account with a blank
      password and demands you change it interactively before doing anything.
      That looks like it kills unattended provisioning outright, and I had
      already started designing around it &mdash; which would have meant putting
      a credential into a public repository or building machinery to inject one
      at provision time.
    </p>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">The fix was to test the assumption instead of designing around it</p>
      <p>Before building anything, I ran the one command that would tell me
        whether the problem was real in the case I cared about: a
        <em>non-interactive</em> SSH, the way a script would actually connect.</p>
      <p>It printed its output and exited. The forced password change is a
        property of interactive logins only. A script never meets it. The
        provisioning script needed no changes at all, and the credential problem
        I was about to solve did not exist.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>What did block automation</strong> showed up in
        the output of that same test, in two lines I had been typing past all
        evening: an SSH host-key confirmation prompt, and a passphrase prompt on
        my own key. Neither stops a human. Both stop a script dead.</p>
    </div>

    <p>
      The host-key prompt is not incidental, and it does not go away. Netinstall
      regenerates the router&rsquo;s SSH host keys on every cycle, so the
      device&rsquo;s identity legitimately changes every single time by design.
      The lazy fix is to disable host-key checking, which throws away a real
      protection to silence a prompt. What it actually wants is a
      <em>separate</em> known-hosts file for the lab, so a provisioning script
      can discard one file per cycle without touching anything else, and
      still refuse a <em>changed</em> key mid-cycle &mdash; which is the case
      that would genuinely mean something is wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      The device end is unattended. What remains is host-side automation hygiene
      on the machine driving it: a lab-only known-hosts file, and a key the
      script can use without a prompt. Those two would have blocked every later
      piece of automation in this lab.
    </p>

    <h2 id="three-back-doors-one-shape">Three back doors, one shape</h2>
    <p>
      The eventual experiment asks whether an agent working on a live router can
      avoid locking itself out. That question is only meaningful if being locked
      out is actually possible, so the provisioning config has to be deliberate
      about every path into the device &mdash; not just the obvious one.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Pi reaches the router through exactly one firewall rule: SSH, from one
      address, on one interface, sitting above a deny-everything-else rule. That
      rule is the surface the experiment is meant to attack. Three other ways in
      turned up, and they are all the same shape: a management path that
      survives the failure being studied.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>MAC-Winbox.</strong> MikroTik&rsquo;s layer-2 management,
        which works with no IP configured anywhere. Left restricted to the LAN
        side, so the Pi has no layer-2 fallback and can genuinely lock itself
        out &mdash; while my desktop, on the other side, keeps it and stays the
        recovery post.</li>
      <li><strong>IPv6 link-local.</strong> The one I nearly missed. RouterOS
        brings up link-local IPv6 automatically, and my config had no IPv6
        firewall rules at all, which means IPv6 input was allowed by default. An
        agent could have destroyed every IPv4 path I had carefully narrowed and
        the Pi would still have connected over IPv6. That failure would not have
        looked like a broken experiment; it would have quietly produced a wrong
        answer.</li>
      <li><strong>RoMON.</strong> Another layer-2 overlay, found while reading an
        unrelated diagnostic. Off by default on this device &mdash; a restrictive
        shipping mode doing me a favour I hadn&rsquo;t asked for.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      I found the second path by applying the question from the first, and the
      third by accident. <strong>&ldquo;Locked out&rdquo; only means something
      once every independent path in has been enumerated</strong>, and there is
      no obvious point at which that enumeration is finished.
    </p>

    <h2 id="a-deferred-item-that-turned-out-not-to-be">A deferred item that turned out not to be deferrable</h2>
    <p>
      RouterOS has a feature called device-mode that gates whole categories of
      functionality, and changing it requires physical confirmation &mdash; a
      power cycle within a five-minute window. You cannot do it over SSH alone,
      which is precisely the point of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      I had noted device-mode as relevant to the <em>later</em> experiment and
      filed it accordingly. It then blocked the current one: the command that
      arms the router for its next wipe cycle is gated behind a device-mode flag,
      and a factory board ships with it off. So the provisioning script&rsquo;s
      final line could not run on a fresh device, and no amount of scripting
      could fix that, because the resolution requires someone to physically pull
      a plug.
    </p>
    <p>
      &ldquo;This only affects a later phase&rdquo; was an assumption I recorded
      as though it were a finding, about the one subsystem whose entire purpose
      is to block things. The decision record now carries the correction in
      place rather than a tidy rewrite.
    </p>
    <p>
      The flag survives a reinstall, so enabling it is a one-time cost per
      device rather than a tax on every cycle. That answer also kept a RouterOS
      version upgrade off the critical path, which is where it would have gone
      if the flag had reset.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-this-does-not-establish">What this does not establish</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>This is not standards-based ZTP.</strong> Real zero-touch
        provisioning has a trust story &mdash; RFC 8572, BRSKI, a certificate
        authority, a device proving what it is before accepting configuration.
        This has none of that. The trust model is that I plugged in the cable
        myself. It is the operational mechanics of unattended provisioning
        without the security substrate that makes it safe at scale.</li>
      <li><strong>One device, one model, one OS version.</strong> A handful of
        cycles on a single board. Nothing here is a claim about how MikroTik
        hardware behaves in general, let alone anyone else&rsquo;s.</li>
      <li><strong>Unattended within a session, not across a reboot.</strong> The
        provisioning host holds its SSH key in an agent that a human has to
        unlock after the Pi restarts. That is a deliberate trade I made against
        a passphrase-free key, and it lands squarely on the experiment this lab
        exists for, since an unattended lab is exactly the scenario where a
        reboot is most likely.</li>
      <li><strong>One thing is unresolved.</strong> The setting that
        re-arms the router lives in the bootloader, not the OS, so it may have
        survived the reinstall on its own rather than being re-applied by my
        script. Both produce identical output. This board is armed either way; a
        truly factory board might not be, and I have not yet run the test that
        distinguishes them.</li>
      <li><strong>The actual experiment is not built.</strong> The self-lockout
        question is the reason this lab exists, and everything above is the
        apparatus for it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="stack">Stack</h2>
    <p>
      MikroTik hEX (RouterOS 7, ARM), Raspberry Pi 5 running Raspberry Pi OS
      from NVMe, MikroTik Netinstall under <code>qemu-user-static</code>, BOOTP
      and TFTP served on an isolated point-to-point link, RouterOS scripting for
      the provisioning configuration, and a shell wrapper so a full cycle is one
      command. The repo is public:
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/netops-lab">github.com/sanlee-ys/netops-lab</a>
      &mdash; the provisioning script, the decision records, and a field log of
      what broke and what the symptoms actually meant are all checkable.
    </p>
    <p>
      The field log is as detailed as the design docs. The two things that cost
      the most time in this build were an Ethernet link that would not come up
      until I disabled a power-saving feature on the host network card, and an
      interface that reported a perfectly correct IP address while no cable was
      attached to it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-it-demonstrates">What it demonstrates</h2>
    <p>
      The recovery mechanism was built before the experiment that needs it. The
      forced-password-change problem would have taken a day to solve properly and
      did not exist; one command established that. The IPv6 hole would have taken
      no time at all to leave in place, and would have invalidated the results
      without a visible failure.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most of what I do professionally is measured on software I can re-run
      until it is right. Hardware is less forgiving about that, and a management
      plane you are reaching through is less forgiving still &mdash; every change
      is potentially the change that ends the session. That is why the lab
      exists.
    </p>
  </main>
  <script is:inline src="../assets/theme.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="../assets/last-updated.js"></script>
</Base>
